Below you’ll find two quick patterns—one for **functional** components (using `useState`) and one for classic **class‑based** components—to “reset” your `userSession` piece of state back to `null`.  
Feel free to copy/paste into your file and tweak as necessary.

---

### 1️⃣ Functional Component (`React.useState`)

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";

export default function MyComponent() {
  const [userSession, setUserSession] = useState<YourType | null>(/* initial value */);

  /* …other logic… */

  /** Reset handler */
  const handleReset = () => {
    setUserSession(null);          // ← clears the session
    // If you also store it somewhere else:
    //   - remove token from cookies/localStorage
    //   - invalidate server-side session via API call
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Render UI based on whether userSession exists */}
      {userSession ? (
        <>
          <p>Welcome back!</p>
          <button onClick={handleReset}>Log out</button>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>No active session.</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### Things to remember:

| What | Why |
|------|-----|
| `setUserSession(null)` | Triggers re-render & updates state |
| Remove persisted data | Prevents stale tokens after logout |
| Optional cleanup effect | e.g., clearing timers when session ends |

If you’re storing the session elsewhere (e.g., Redux, Context), make sure those stores get cleared too.

---

### 2️⃣ Class Component (`this.state`, `this.setState`)

```jsx
import React from "react";

class MyClass extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      userSession: /* initial value */,
    };
  }

  /* ... other methods ... */

  /**
   * Resets the userSession state to null.
   */
  resetSession = () => {
    this.setState({ userSession: null });
    // Optionally clean up external storage here
  };

  render() {
    const { userSession } = this.state;
    return (
      <div>
        {userSession ? (
          <>
            <h3>Hello again!</h3>
            <button onClick={this.resetSession}>Logout</button>
          </>
        ) : (
          <h3>You’re logged out.</h3>
        )}
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default MyClass;
```

Again, don’t forget to wipe any stored auth tokens or side effects outside of React’s state.

---

## Bonus: Clearing Persisted Tokens

Most apps keep JWTs or similar tokens in either `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, or cookies. After setting the state to `null`, consider adding something like:

```js
// Example – removing a JWT from localStorage
window.localStorage.removeItem("authToken");

// Or clearing a cookie
document.cookie =
  "token=; expires=Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 UTC; path=/;";
```

Or, if you have an async sign-out endpoint:

```ts
async function logOutAndClear() {
  await fetch("/api/logout", { method: "POST" }); // optional server invalidation
  setUserSession(null);
  window.localStorage.removeItem("authToken");
}
```

That way both client‑side state and persistent storage stay in sync.

